WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.555
Transport validation answers the question, can this payload be understood as the request shape for this endpoint.

2
00:00:07.555 --> 00:00:14.332
Domain validation answers a deeper question: does this request make sense for the business operation.

3
00:00:14.332 --> 00:00:25.003
Required fields, optional fields, enums, nested objects, arrays, IDs, dates, and monetary values all need structural checks, but structure alone is not enough.

4
00:00:25.003 --> 00:00:29.053
A value can be well formed and still violate an important rule.

5
00:00:29.053 --> 00:00:31.779
Consider dates, money, and identifiers.

6
00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:37.154
A date string may parse correctly but represent a time outside the allowed window.

7
00:00:37.154 --> 00:00:44.631
A monetary amount may be numeric but have too many decimal places, use the wrong currency, or exceed a policy limit.

8
00:00:44.631 --> 00:00:49.850
An ID may match a UUID format but refer to a record the user is not allowed to touch.

9
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:59.975
Validation should separate format checks from authorization and business checks while making sure all of them occur before the operation changes state.

10
00:00:59.975 --> 00:01:03.169
Cross-field validation is where many bugs hide.

11
00:01:03.169 --> 00:01:05.973
A start date should come before an end date.

12
00:01:05.973 --> 00:01:09.634
A discount should be compatible with the selected plan.

13
00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:13.217
A shipping method should be valid for the destination.

14
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:21.084
An array should not exceed a safe size, contain duplicates when uniqueness is required, or mix incompatible item types.

15
00:01:21.084 --> 00:01:30.508
These rules usually belong near the domain service or domain object because they describe what the operation means, not just how JSON is shaped.

16
00:01:30.508 --> 00:01:38.842
A strong TypeScript design uses validated transport objects as inputs to domain construction, not as the domain model itself.

17
00:01:38.842 --> 00:01:42.971
The transport layer can reject malformed or unexpected input.

18
00:01:42.971 --> 00:01:48.812
The domain layer can enforce invariants that must always be true inside the application.

19
00:01:48.812 --> 00:01:53.408
Keeping those concerns separate makes code easier to test and review.

20
00:01:53.408 --> 00:02:02.755
It also helps prevent a change in an API contract from accidentally weakening a core rule about money, identity, ownership, or workflow state.
